    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d110">

                <pb facs="0232" n="133" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg133"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.11/2060</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Latin
                        American Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Duggan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-23">April 23, 1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I do not believe it would be wise to make any official pronouncement “out of the
                    blue” with regard to the attitude of this Government towards the recognition
                    features of the 1923 treaty. Such action would attract undue attention and give
                    rise to great speculation. It seems to me it would be better to wait until some
                    favorable occasion arises. Mr. Beaulac suggests that such an occasion might
                    arise at the proposed peace conference, although this does not seem very likely
                    to me.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Laurence Duggan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
